  IV. THE CRITICAL PROPERTY: ONE NEW PRIME PER STEP
  --------------------------------------------------

  Canonical chain analysis:

  Step 0: n=1. Divisors of 1: {1}. d+1=2 prime. VS={2}. Prod=2.
    NEW PRIME: 2. (1 new prime)

  Step 1: n=2. Divisors of 2: {1,2}. d+1={2,3}. VS={2,3}. Prod=6.
    NEW PRIME: 3. (1 new prime)

  Step 2: n=6. Divisors of 6: {1,2,3,6}. d+1={2,3,4,7}.
    Primes: {2,3,7}. VS={2,3,7}. Prod=42.
    NEW PRIME: 7. (1 new prime)

  Step 3: n=42. Divisors of 42: {1,2,3,6,7,14,21,42}.
    d+1 = {2,3,4,7,8,15,22,43}.
    Primes: {2,3,7,43}. VS={2,3,7,43}. Prod=1806.
    NEW PRIME: 43. (1 new prime)

  Step 4: n=1806. VS primes = {2,3,7,43}. Prod=1806. FIXED POINT.
    NEW PRIMES: none. (0 new primes)

  The canonical chain adds EXACTLY ONE PRIME per step until stabilizing.
  This is the minimal growth trajectory.

  WHY 42 attracts exactly one new prime:
  Divisors of 42 contribute d+1 = {2,3,4,7,8,15,22,43}
  Already known: {2,3,7}
  New candidates: {4(=2^2), 8(=2^3), 15(=3*5), 22(=2*11), 43}
  Composite: {4, 8, 15, 22}
  PRIME: {43}
  So 43 is the ONLY new prime. This requires 43=42+1 to be prime!

  Similarly for 6: d+1 = {2,3,4,7}. Known: {2,3}. New: {4(composite), 7(PRIME)}.
  7 is the only new prime. Requires 7=6+1 to be prime!

  The chain works because at each step, n+1 is prime and all other
  new d+1 values are composite. This is the Sylvester condition:
  a_{k+1} = P_k + 1 is prime.

  V. THE SYLVESTER PRIMALITY CONDITION
  --------------------------------------------------

  The canonical chain adds one prime per step IFF:
  P_k + 1 is prime, AND
  no divisor d of P_k with d not in {1, P_{k-1}} gives a new prime d+1.

  Let's verify the second condition is automatic:
  If P_k = 2*3*7*...p_k, then every proper divisor d is a product of
  a subset of {2,3,7,...,p_k}. So d+1 = (some product)+1.
  For d+1 to be a NEW prime not already in the set,
  d+1 must not divide P_k (otherwise d+1 would already be a factor).

  n=6: divisors giving primes d+1: [2, 3, 7]
  n=42: divisors giving primes d+1: [2, 3, 7, 43]
  n=1806: divisors giving primes d+1: [2, 3, 7, 43]

  VI. THE NUMBER THEORY BEHIND THE FIXED POINT
  --------------------------------------------------

  1806 = 2 * 3 * 7 * 43 is a VS fixed point because:
  (1) Every prime factor p of 1806 has (p-1)|1806:
      1|1806, 2|1806, 6|1806, 42|1806. All true.
  (2) No OTHER prime q has (q-1)|1806:
      Need (q-1) | 1806 = 2*3*7*43 and q prime, q not in {2,3,7,43}.
      q=5: (q-1)=4 | 1806? NO (remainder 2)
      q=11: (q-1)=10 | 1806? NO (remainder 6)
      q=13: (q-1)=12 | 1806? NO (remainder 6)
      q=19: (q-1)=18 | 1806? NO (remainder 6)
      q=23: (q-1)=22 | 1806? NO (remainder 2)
      q=31: (q-1)=30 | 1806? NO (remainder 6)
      q=37: (q-1)=36 | 1806? NO (remainder 6)
      q=41: (q-1)=40 | 1806? NO (remainder 6)
      q=59: (q-1)=58 | 1806? NO (remainder 8)
      q=61: (q-1)=60 | 1806? NO (remainder 6)

  Checking all primes q < 200:
  (No problems found => 1806 is confirmed as VS fixed point)

  d+1 for each divisor d of 1806:
    d=    1: d+1=    2 PRIME (already a factor of 1806)
    d=    2: d+1=    3 PRIME (already a factor of 1806)
    d=    3: d+1=    4 = 2^2
    d=    6: d+1=    7 PRIME (already a factor of 1806)
    d=    7: d+1=    8 = 2^3
    d=   14: d+1=   15 = 3*5
    d=   21: d+1=   22 = 2*11
    d=   42: d+1=   43 PRIME (already a factor of 1806)
    d=   43: d+1=   44 = 2^2*11
    d=   86: d+1=   87 = 3*29
    d=  129: d+1=  130 = 2*5*13
    d=  258: d+1=  259 = 7*37
    d=  301: d+1=  302 = 2*151
    d=  602: d+1=  603 = 3^2*67
    d=  903: d+1=  904 = 2^3*113
    d= 1806: d+1= 1807 = 13*139

  Every d+1 that is prime is already in {2,3,7,43}.
  Every other d+1 is composite.
  The set {2,3,7,43} is SELF-CONTAINED under the VS operation.

  VII. CONNECTION TO HURWITZ
  --------------------------------------------------

  {2,3,7} = primes dividing disc(Hurwitz quaternions) = 42
  {2,3,7,43} = primes dividing denom(B_42) = 1806
  43 = 42+1 = disc(Hurwitz)+1

  The Hurwitz primes are NOT a VS fixed point.
  They need 43 to complete the self-selecting set.
  42 is the TRANSITION: the bridge from {2,3,7} to {2,3,7,43}.

  Interpretation: The Hurwitz quaternion algebra (ramified at 2,3,7)
  is INCOMPLETE from the von Staudt perspective.
  It needs one more prime (43 = 42+1) to achieve self-consistency.
  The VS fixed point 1806 = 42 * 43 is the COMPLETED Hurwitz system.

